            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "TRIANGLES"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.104,
            0.897,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "81"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.139,
            0.508,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, from (1), (2) and (3), we have :"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.171,
            0.44,
            0.212
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "{ \\frac { \\mathrm { A D } } { \\mathrm { D B } } } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { A E } } { \\mathrm { E C } } }"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.222,
            0.9,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is the converse of this theorem also true (For the meaning of converse, see Appendix 1)? To examine this, let us perform the following activity:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.273,
            0.547,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 3 :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Draw an angle XAY on your notebook and on ray AX, mark points "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf { B } _ { 1 } ,   \\mathbf { B } _ { 2 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf { B } _ { 3 } ,   \\mathbf { B } _ { 4 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and B such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AB}_{1}=\\mathrm{B}_{1} \\mathrm{B}_{2}=\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{B}_{3}="
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf { \\widetilde { B _ { 3 } } } \\mathbf { \\widetilde { B _ { 4 } } } = \\mathbf { B _ { 4 } } \\mathbf { B }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.358,
            0.547,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, on ray AY, mark points "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "C_{1}, C_{2}, C_{3}, C_{4}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and C such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AC}_{1} = \\mathrm{C}_{1}\\mathrm{C}_{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "C_{2}C_{3}=C_{3}C_{4}=C_{4}C"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Then join "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { B _ { 1 } C _ { 1 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and BC (see Fig. 6.11)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.574,
            0.27,
            0.895,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.574,
                0.27,
                0.895,
                0.458
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.474,
            0.247,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.457,
            0.652,
            0.502
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{1}}{\\mathrm{B}_{1}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{1}}{\\mathrm{C}_{1}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(\\text { Each equal } \\cup \\frac{1}{4}\\right)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.512,
            0.826,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can also see that lines "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{B_{1}C_{1}}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and BC are parallel to each other, i.e.,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.537,
            0.896,
            0.559
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "B _{1} C _{1}\\parallel  BC\\tag{1}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.563,
            0.665,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, by joining "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2}, \\mathrm{B}_{3} \\mathrm{C}_{3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { B _ { 4 } C _ { 4 } } ;"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " you can see that:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.299,
            0.595,
            0.896,
            0.642
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{2}}{\\mathrm{B}_{2}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{2}}{\\mathrm{C}_{2}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(=\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\text { and }\\mathrm{B}_{2}\\mathrm{C}_{2}\\parallel\\mathrm{BC}\\tag{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.656,
            0.896,
            0.701
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_14.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{3}}{\\mathrm{B}_{3}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{3}}{\\mathrm{C}_{3}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(=\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\text { and }\\mathrm{B}_{3}\\mathrm{C}_{3}\\parallel\\mathrm{BC}\\tag{3}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.3,
            0.715,
            0.896,
            0.761
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{4}}{\\mathrm{B}_{4}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{4}}{\\mathrm{C}_{4}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(=\\frac{4}{1}\\right)\\text { and }\\mathrm{B}_{4}\\mathrm{C}_{4}\\parallel\\mathrm{BC}\\tag{4}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.773,
            0.901,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From (1), (2), (3) and (4), it can be observed that if a line divides two sides of a triangle in the same ratio, then the line is parallel to the third side."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.819,
            0.903,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can repeat this activity by drawing any angle XAY of different measure and taking any number of equal parts on arms AX and AY . Each time, you will arrive at the same result. Thus, we obtain the following theorem, which is the converse of Theorem 6.1:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
